# Code Quality

## Code Comments

**General Principle:** Code should be self-documenting. Comments should only be used when necessary to explain "why" something is done, not "what" the code does.

### Rules:

- **Avoid obvious comments** - Don't comment on what the code clearly shows
- **Prefer self-documenting code** - Use descriptive variable names, clear function names, and well-structured code instead of comments
- **Only comment when necessary** - Comments should explain:
  - Complex business logic or non-obvious decisions
  - Workarounds for bugs or limitations
  - Performance optimizations that aren't obvious
  - References to external documentation or specifications

### Bad Examples:

```typescript
{
  /* Hero Section */
}
<section>...</section>;

{
  /* Search Input */
}
<SearchInput />;

// Set the value
setValue(value);

// If still loading auth state, wait
if (isAuthLoading) {
  return;
}

// If not authenticated, redirect to sign-in
if (!isAuthenticated) {
  router.push("/signin");
}
```

### Good Examples:

```typescript
<section>...</section>;

<SearchInput />;

setValue(value);

if (isAuthLoading) {
  return;
}

if (!isAuthenticated) {
  router.push('/signin');
}

// Mock data for MVP (will be replaced with API data)
const popularStyles = [...];

// Calculate budget band from minSpend (rough estimate)
const budgetBand = artist.minSpend < 200 ? 1 : 2;

// Workaround for Next.js Image optimization issue
<Image unoptimized {...props} />
```

### ESLint Enforcement:

The project uses ESLint rules to enforce this:

- `capitalized-comments`: Enforces capitalization for meaningful comments
- `no-warning-comments`: Warns about TODO/FIXME comments

## Scope

These rules apply to:

- All files in `src/Client.Operations/src/components/` and subdirectories
- All component-related files (`.tsx`, `.ts`, `.css`, etc.)
